# Interactive D3.js Diagram Pattern

Reusable pattern for creating interactive, collapsible tree diagrams in self-contained HTML files. Developed through the ACM Full Architecture diagram project (270 nodes, 21 depth levels). Use this pattern for any hierarchical visualization.

## When to Use

- Architecture diagrams (deployment chains, component hierarchies)
- Workflow visualizations (provisioning flows, request paths)
- Any tree-structured data that benefits from progressive disclosure

## When NOT to Use

- Sequence diagrams (use Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` instead)
- Simple diagrams with <15 nodes (Mermaid is simpler)
- Non-hierarchical data (network graphs, ER diagrams)

## Diagram Type Selection

Choose the rendering approach based on the data shape:

| Data Shape | Renderer | Features |
|------------|----------|----------|
| **Hierarchy** (parent → children) | `renderTree()` | Collapsible D3 tree, +/- buttons, expand/collapse all |
| **Sequential workflow** (step → step → step) | `renderFlow()` | Left-to-right flow with arrows, NO expand/collapse |
| **Mixed** (multiple diagram types in one page) | Tab bar + per-tab renderer | Each tab gets the appropriate renderer |

**Key rule**: Sequential workflows (pipelines, CI/CD stages, processing steps) must use flow layout, NOT tree layout. Pipelines are not hierarchies -- forcing them into a tree misrepresents the data and makes the expand/collapse affordance confusing. Trees are for containment hierarchies (component → sub-components, skill tiers → skills).

